1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,560
This is Doing and Feeling the Long Version, Episode 3, Intelligence Formally.

2
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:11,860
In 2006, the computer scientist Marcus Hutter put up prize money for an odd challenge.

3
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:16,540
Compress 100 megabytes of English Wikipedia as small as possible.

4
00:00:16,820 --> 00:00:24,500
The program that decompresses it counts toward the total size, so you cannot cheat by hiding the text inside the decompressor.

5
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:31,420
Since 2020, the challenge has used a full gigabyte, with a prize fund of up to half a million euros,

6
00:00:31,740 --> 00:00:36,800
and the record fell three times in 2026, most recently in July.

7
00:00:37,160 --> 00:00:40,460
Why would zipping Wikipedia have anything to do with intelligence?

8
00:00:40,820 --> 00:00:44,820
The answer connects information theory, the foundations of statistics,

9
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:50,960
a mathematical definition of intelligence, and the training objective of every large language model.

10
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:55,440
Last episode, intelligence took many forms in many bodies.

11
00:00:55,900 --> 00:01:01,480
This one asks whether there's a definition that does not depend on any species, body, or test.

12
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:02,900
Here's the argument.

13
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:08,940
The best formal definitions tie intelligence to prediction and compression across many environments.

14
00:01:09,320 --> 00:01:15,400
But every definition builds in assumptions about what counts as simple or about which tasks matter,

15
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:17,560
and no learner is best at everything.

16
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:21,400
That is why measuring machine intelligence is so contested,

17
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:25,600
and why a score can change dramatically depending on how a model is run.

18
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:28,220
Start with Alan Turing.

19
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:31,940
In 1950, he proposed replacing the question,

20
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:37,040
can machines think, which he considered too meaningless to deserve discussion, with a game.

21
00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:41,960
An interrogator converses by text with a hidden human and a hidden machine.

22
00:01:42,340 --> 00:01:45,380
If the interrogator cannot reliably tell which is which,

23
00:01:45,380 --> 00:01:48,440
the machine has done everything we could reasonably ask.

24
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:52,140
The imitation game is operational, which is its virtue.

25
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:55,900
It is also a test of human likeness, which is its weakness.

26
00:01:56,220 --> 00:02:02,080
In 2025, Cameron Jones and Benjamin Burgin ran a pre-registered version

27
00:02:02,080 --> 00:02:04,520
with Turing's original three-party design.

28
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:09,000
Judges chatted for five minutes with a human and a machine at the same time,

29
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:10,880
then picked which one was human.

30
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:15,720
GPT-4.5, prompted to adopt a particular human-like persona,

31
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:21,240
was judged to be the human 73% of the time, more often than the real humans were.

32
00:02:21,580 --> 00:02:26,480
A meta model given the same persona prompt was picked 56% of the time.

33
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:34,380
Baseline systems, GPT-4.0 and the 1960s chatbot Eliza, were picked only about a fifth of the time.

34
00:02:34,660 --> 00:02:39,880
So both of the systems that passed were playing a character someone had written for them.

35
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:45,940
lesson. What the test measured was how well a system could play a particular kind of human.

36
00:02:46,260 --> 00:02:51,360
That's a real capability, and a socially important one, given what it implies about

37
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:55,600
who you might be talking to online. But it is not a definition of intelligence.

38
00:02:56,680 --> 00:03:02,260
The deeper story starts with Claude Shannon. In his 1948 theory of communication,

39
00:03:02,660 --> 00:03:08,800
information is surprise. An event with probability p carries minus log 2 of p bits.

40
00:03:09,260 --> 00:03:13,960
In a 1951 paper, Shannon estimated the information content of English

41
00:03:13,960 --> 00:03:18,520
by asking people to guess the next letter of a text, one letter at a time.

42
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:23,520
People were good at it because they understood the language and the world the text described.

43
00:03:24,060 --> 00:03:28,040
Shannon was using people's knowledge of the language as a measuring instrument.

44
00:03:28,300 --> 00:03:32,280
The better they guessed, the less information each new letter carried.

45
00:03:32,700 --> 00:03:33,720
Here's the key link.

46
00:03:33,880 --> 00:03:37,300
A technique called arithmetic coding can encode a symbol

47
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:44,600
p using very nearly minus log 2 of p bits. So the better your model predicts the next symbol,

48
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:50,680
the fewer bits you need to encode it. Prediction and compression are the same problem. Train a

49
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:55,600
model to minimize prediction error, measured as cross-entropy, and you are literally training

50
00:03:55,600 --> 00:04:01,740
it to compress. Which is why language model loss is reported in bits or Nats per token.

51
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:08,080
Per training is compression with extra steps. And researchers at DeepMind made that literal

52
00:04:09,340 --> 00:04:16,020
paper titled Language Modeling is Compression. They used a large model trained mostly on text

53
00:04:16,020 --> 00:04:21,660
as a general-purpose compressor, and it squeezed image patches and audio smaller than the standard

54
00:04:21,660 --> 00:04:28,640
specialized formats, PNG, and FLAC. The caveat matters. Count the size of the model itself,

55
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:34,840
and it loses badly because the model is enormous. But the result shows how much general structure

56
00:04:34,840 --> 00:04:41,320
a good predictor absorbs. If good prediction means good compression, what is the best possible

57
00:04:41,320 --> 00:04:47,920
predictor? In the 1960s, Ray Solomonoff gave a remarkable answer. Consider every computer

58
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:53,040
program that could have produced the data you've seen so far. Weight each program by two to the

59
00:04:53,040 --> 00:04:58,720
minus its length in bits so that shorter programs count for much more. Then predict the next

60
00:04:58,720 --> 00:05:04,560
observation by averaging over what all those programs would output next. This is Occam's

61
00:05:04,560 --> 00:05:09,740
razor made mathematical. The length of the shortest program that produces a given string

62
00:05:09,740 --> 00:05:16,380
is called its Kalmogorov complexity, after Andrei Kalmogorov, who developed the idea independently,

63
00:05:16,680 --> 00:05:22,560
as did Gregory Chaitin. Solomanov showed that if the data come from any computable process at all,

64
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:26,940
his predictor's total error is bounded by the complexity of that process.

65
00:05:28,720 --> 00:05:34,780
world, and no computable predictor can beat it by more than a constant. The catch is that it is

66
00:05:34,780 --> 00:05:40,900
uncomputable. Evaluating it would require knowing which programs ever halt, which is impossible in

67
00:05:40,900 --> 00:05:47,360
general. So it is a gold standard, not an algorithm. But it gives a precise sense in which intelligence

68
00:05:47,360 --> 00:05:53,300
and compression are linked. The best predictor is the one that finds the shortest explanation of the

69
00:05:53,300 --> 00:06:01,220
data. In 2007, Shane Legge, who would later co-found DeepMind, and Marcus Hutter used this

70
00:06:01,220 --> 00:06:07,080
machinery to define intelligence itself. They first gathered around 70 informal definitions

71
00:06:07,080 --> 00:06:12,680
from psychologists, AI researchers, and dictionaries, and distilled their common core.

72
00:06:13,120 --> 00:06:18,280
Intelligence measures an agent's ability to achieve goals in a wide range of environments.

73
00:06:18,740 --> 00:06:20,100
Then they formalized it.

74
00:06:20,640 --> 00:06:23,740
Imagine every computable environment an agent could face,

75
00:06:23,920 --> 00:06:26,720
each one providing observations and rewards.

76
00:06:27,040 --> 00:06:30,140
Measure the reward the agent can expect to earn in each.

77
00:06:30,320 --> 00:06:32,780
Then take a weighted sum over all of them,

78
00:06:33,140 --> 00:06:37,120
weighting each environment by 2 to the minus its Kalmogorov complexity

79
00:06:37,120 --> 00:06:39,760
so that simpler environments count more.

80
00:06:40,240 --> 00:06:42,860
That sum is the agent's universal intelligence.

81
00:06:43,420 --> 00:06:46,260
Hutter had earlier defined an agent called ICSI

82
00:06:46,260 --> 00:06:49,180
that maximizes exactly this kind of quantity

83
00:06:49,180 --> 00:06:52,320
by combining Solomanov's predictor with planning.

84
00:06:52,800 --> 00:06:55,960
Consider every program consistent with what you've seen,

85
00:06:56,140 --> 00:06:57,380
weighted by simplicity,

86
00:06:57,740 --> 00:07:01,820
and choose the action with the best expected outcome across all of them.

87
00:07:01,940 --> 00:07:06,120
In that precise sense, ICSI is the most intelligent possible agent.

88
00:07:06,440 --> 00:07:08,120
It is also uncomputable.

89
00:07:08,980 --> 00:07:10,160
Approximations exist.

90
00:07:10,420 --> 00:07:14,620
In 2011, a team led by Joel Venice built one

91
00:07:16,260 --> 00:07:21,940
small problems, the largest aversion of Pac-Man in which the agent could sense only its immediate

92
00:07:21,940 --> 00:07:28,080
surroundings. But there's a known problem with all of this. The answer depends on which universal

93
00:07:28,080 --> 00:07:33,520
Turing machine you use to measure program length. Yes, and it is not a technicality.

94
00:07:34,200 --> 00:07:39,080
Kalmogorov complexity is only defined up to a constant that depends on that reference machine.

95
00:07:39,540 --> 00:07:43,800
In 2015, Jan Laika and Hutter showed how much it matters.

96
00:07:44,180 --> 00:07:48,700
With an unlucky choice of reference machine, Ike-C can behave very badly,

97
00:07:48,920 --> 00:07:52,820
and Legg and Hutter's measure becomes, in their word, subjective.

98
00:07:53,260 --> 00:07:55,940
Worse, by one natural standard of optimality,

99
00:07:56,380 --> 00:08:01,600
every possible policy counts as optimal across the class of all computable environments,

100
00:08:01,960 --> 00:08:03,840
which makes that standard empty.

101
00:08:04,660 --> 00:08:10,000
Universal intelligence is universal only relative to a choice of what counts as simple.

102
00:08:10,980 --> 00:08:15,940
That connects to one of the deepest results in this area, the no-free-lunch theorems,

103
00:08:16,160 --> 00:08:22,180
proved by David Walpert in the mid-1990s, for learning on his own and for optimization with

104
00:08:22,180 --> 00:08:28,140
William Macready. Averaged over all possible problems, every learning or optimization method

105
00:08:28,140 --> 00:08:33,200
performs exactly as well as every other. Averaged across every conceivable world,

106
00:08:33,500 --> 00:08:39,660
no method beats random guessing. So there's no such thing as general intelligence in the absolute.

107
00:08:40,120 --> 00:08:45,160
Not in the absolute. An intelligence is good across a particular distribution of problems,

108
00:08:45,160 --> 00:08:49,060
and it gets there by having assumptions, priors, that fit that distribution.

109
00:08:49,840 --> 00:08:54,680
Legge and Hutter's simplicity weighting is one such assumption, that the world tends to be simple.

110
00:08:55,340 --> 00:09:00,460
Evolution built different assumptions into crows, octopuses, and us, fitted to our different worlds.

111
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:07,100
So whenever someone calls a system generally intelligent, the right follow-up question is, general across what?

112
00:09:08,340 --> 00:09:17,660
In 2019, François Chalet, then at Google, published a long paper called On the Measure of Intelligence that builds on exactly that point.

113
00:09:17,980 --> 00:09:20,920
His first move is to separate skill from intelligence.

114
00:09:21,660 --> 00:09:29,780
Skill at a task can be bought in two ways, by building in prior knowledge or by training on vast amounts of task-specific experience.

115
00:09:30,200 --> 00:09:35,640
A chess engine is enormously skilled at chess, and that tells you little about its intelligence.

116
00:09:36,100 --> 00:09:39,700
So Chalet defines intelligence as skill acquisition efficiency.

117
00:09:39,980 --> 00:09:49,320
How efficiently a system turns its priors and its experience into skill on new tasks, weighted by how hard those tasks are to generalize to.

118
00:09:50,660 --> 00:09:56,960
consequence. Two systems with equal skill can differ greatly in intelligence if one needed far

119
00:09:56,960 --> 00:10:02,660
more data. On Chalet's measure, a system that learned from trillions of words what a child

120
00:10:02,660 --> 00:10:08,540
learns from millions is showing a great deal of skill and not much efficiency. To compare fairly

121
00:10:08,540 --> 00:10:13,300
with people, Chalet proposed controlling for priors, and he turned to the developmental

122
00:10:13,300 --> 00:10:19,060
psychologist Elizabeth Spelke's work on core knowledge, a small set of systems that infants

123
00:10:21,140 --> 00:10:24,940
for agents and their goals, for number, and for geometry.

124
00:10:25,540 --> 00:10:27,620
A fair test of general intelligence

125
00:10:27,620 --> 00:10:31,360
should require only those priors, plus the ability to learn.

126
00:10:31,640 --> 00:10:34,900
So he built one, the abstraction and reasoning corpus,

127
00:10:35,020 --> 00:10:35,560
or arc.

128
00:10:35,960 --> 00:10:38,840
Each task shows a few examples of colored grids

129
00:10:38,840 --> 00:10:42,160
being transformed and asks for the output on a new input.

130
00:10:42,540 --> 00:10:45,840
Every task follows a different rule designed to be new.

131
00:10:46,220 --> 00:10:49,420
Most people solve most of them, usually within a few minutes.

132
00:10:50,920 --> 00:10:56,480
For years, AI systems did terribly on ARC, even as they aced standardized exams.

133
00:10:57,200 --> 00:11:04,720
Competitions in 2020 and 2024 produced steady progress through program search and test time training,

134
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:09,020
in which a model is briefly fine-tuned on each task's own examples.

135
00:11:09,380 --> 00:11:18,720
Then, in December 2024, a preview of OpenAI's O3 reasoning model scored about 76% on a held-out set,

136
00:11:18,720 --> 00:11:23,220
using six samples per task at roughly $26 a task.

137
00:11:23,400 --> 00:11:28,900
With over 1,000 samples per task and around 170 times the computing cost,

138
00:11:29,160 --> 00:11:30,980
it reached about 88%.

139
00:11:30,980 --> 00:11:33,700
Two caveats belong with those numbers.

140
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:38,540
The tested model had been trained on three-quarters of ARC's public training set,

141
00:11:38,680 --> 00:11:42,320
and the model OpenAI later released was a different version.

142
00:11:42,520 --> 00:11:47,400
A second, harder version, ARC-G i2, launched in March 2025,

143
00:11:48,720 --> 00:11:54,900
model scored essentially zero on it. At the end of that year's competition, the winning open entry,

144
00:11:55,240 --> 00:12:02,240
called Envy Arc, scored about 24% at around 20 cents a task. The best verified commercial model

145
00:12:02,240 --> 00:12:09,640
was Claude Opus 4.5 at about 38%. That is a model from Anthropic, the company that makes the model

146
00:12:09,640 --> 00:12:15,320
riding this series. A refinement harness built by a company called Poetic, wrapped around Google's

147
00:12:15,320 --> 00:12:22,920
Gemini 3 Pro reached 54% at about $30 a task. And the competition's paper award went to a tiny

148
00:12:22,920 --> 00:12:29,500
recursive model with 7 million parameters and two layers, which scored 45% on the first version of

149
00:12:29,500 --> 00:12:36,980
ARK and 8% on the second. 7 million parameters, beating several far larger language models from

150
00:12:36,980 --> 00:12:43,040
earlier that year. That says something about what scale is buying. It says that on this narrow kind

151
00:12:43,040 --> 00:12:48,500
of task, a small network with the right structure, trained on the puzzles themselves, can outdo much

152
00:12:48,500 --> 00:12:55,960
larger general models. Then came ARC, a GI3, launched in March 2026, which abandons static

153
00:12:55,960 --> 00:13:02,420
puzzles for interactive games with no instructions. A system has to explore, work out what the game is,

154
00:13:02,500 --> 00:13:08,020
and win, and it is scored on how efficiently it acts. At launch, human testers solved all of the

155
00:13:08,020 --> 00:13:14,660
games, and Frontier AI systems scored around half of 1%. Meanwhile, the older test was nearly

156
00:13:14,660 --> 00:13:21,140
saturated. By July, ARK's verified results put Claude Opus 5, another anthropic model,

157
00:13:21,580 --> 00:13:31,380
at about 90% on ARK a GI2 and about 30% on the new games. In September 2026, OpenAI's GPT-6 Astra

158
00:13:31,380 --> 00:13:37,760
scored about 63% under ARK's standard test harness and nearly 100% under a different harness,

159
00:13:38,020 --> 00:13:39,840
a so-called provider adapter.

160
00:13:40,240 --> 00:13:42,320
Even with the reasoning setting matched,

161
00:13:42,560 --> 00:13:44,760
it was about 63 against 99.

162
00:13:45,240 --> 00:13:47,720
The ARC prize organizers were explicit

163
00:13:47,720 --> 00:13:49,340
that this was not AGI.

164
00:13:50,100 --> 00:13:51,920
63 versus 100.

165
00:13:52,380 --> 00:13:54,200
Same model, same games.

166
00:13:54,500 --> 00:13:56,580
Whose intelligence is being measured,

167
00:13:56,800 --> 00:13:58,720
the models or the model plus

168
00:13:58,720 --> 00:14:00,200
whatever was built around it?

169
00:14:00,420 --> 00:14:02,040
That's exactly the question,

170
00:14:02,040 --> 00:14:03,440
and it generalizes.

171
00:14:03,840 --> 00:14:06,060
Tools, retries, memory,

172
00:14:08,020 --> 00:14:13,240
built harnesses can change a score dramatically, which brings us to the science of measurement

173
00:14:13,240 --> 00:14:20,220
itself. Measuring AI capability has become its own discipline, and it is harder than it looks.

174
00:14:20,900 --> 00:14:26,520
Benchmarks saturate, a test that once separated systems ends up with everyone near the ceiling.

175
00:14:27,160 --> 00:14:32,680
Benchmarks leak. Test questions find their way into training data, so models can score well

176
00:14:32,680 --> 00:14:38,840
partly by memory, a modern clever hands. When a team led by Hu Zhang wrote a fresh set of grade

177
00:14:38,840 --> 00:14:44,820
school math problems matched to a popular benchmark, some models' accuracy dropped by up to

178
00:14:44,820 --> 00:14:50,940
8%, and the size of the drop tracked how readily a model could reproduce the original test items.

179
00:14:51,300 --> 00:14:56,800
Many models, especially at the frontier, showed little sign of overfitting, which is worth saying

180
00:14:56,800 --> 00:15:03,060
too. And benchmarks invite optimization, which triggers Goodhart's law, in the anthropologist

181
00:15:03,060 --> 00:15:08,680
Marilyn Strathern's well-known phrasing, when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good

182
00:15:08,680 --> 00:15:14,400
measure. There's a deeper problem, one that episode one would recognize, construct validity.

183
00:15:14,720 --> 00:15:23,540
In 2025, a team led by Andrew Bean at Oxford reviewed 445 language model benchmarks. Only

184
00:15:23,540 --> 00:15:29,540
about 16% use statistical tests to compare models and roughly half set out to measure an abstract

185
00:15:29,540 --> 00:15:35,980
concept, like reasoning or safety, without clearly defining it. Psychometrics spent a

186
00:15:35,980 --> 00:15:40,920
century learning to ask what a test measures and whether it measures the same thing across groups.

187
00:15:41,420 --> 00:15:48,260
Much of AI evaluation is starting that work now. Researchers have also tried to define the target

188
00:15:48,260 --> 00:15:55,180
itself. One approach defines general intelligence by comparison with people. In October 2025,

189
00:15:55,800 --> 00:16:02,460
33 researchers led by Dan Hendricks proposed defining AGI as matching the cognitive versatility

190
00:16:02,460 --> 00:16:08,700
and proficiency of a well-educated adult. Across 10 domains drawn from the cattle horn carol model,

191
00:16:09,060 --> 00:16:16,500
we met in episode one, weighted equally. They scored GPT-4 at about 27 percent and GPT-5

192
00:16:18,320 --> 00:16:23,340
with long-term memory storage the weakest area, where both models scored zero.

193
00:16:23,880 --> 00:16:29,120
One natural objection is that this measures how human-like a profile of abilities is,

194
00:16:29,340 --> 00:16:31,620
which may not be the same thing as intelligence.

195
00:16:32,020 --> 00:16:34,600
A different approach measures autonomy directly.

196
00:16:34,920 --> 00:16:38,100
The research group meter estimates the length of tasks,

197
00:16:38,340 --> 00:16:40,800
measured by how long they take skilled people,

198
00:16:41,060 --> 00:16:44,580
that AI agents can complete with 50% reliability.

199
00:16:44,580 --> 00:16:51,980
By their estimates, that Horizon doubled roughly every seven months between 2019 and 2025,

200
00:16:52,600 --> 00:16:55,400
and closer to every four months since 2023.

201
00:16:55,900 --> 00:17:01,280
The most capable systems measured in 2026 are put at more than a working day.

202
00:17:01,480 --> 00:17:08,560
But Meter itself warns that measurements above about 16 hours are unreliable with its current set of test tasks.

203
00:17:08,720 --> 00:17:13,000
And a third approach borrows the psychometrics of episode 1 directly.

204
00:17:14,580 --> 00:17:20,220
In 2023, David Elitch analyzed more than 1,200 language models on a public leaderboard

205
00:17:20,220 --> 00:17:25,960
and found that a single factor explained about 85% of the variation in their benchmark scores.

206
00:17:26,160 --> 00:17:28,720
A strong machine, positive manifold.

207
00:17:29,100 --> 00:17:36,460
In April 2026, the complexity scientist David Krakauer asked how that structure changes over time

208
00:17:36,460 --> 00:17:40,500
in a paper titled The Rise and Fall of G in AGI.

209
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:48,200
Across 39 models, on the 8 most widely reported benchmarks, scores correlated strongly, around 0.8 on average.

210
00:17:48,380 --> 00:17:56,260
And on one core battery, the first factor explained about 92% of the variation in 2023 and early 2024.

211
00:17:56,820 --> 00:18:04,800
It had begun to slip by mid-2024, and after reasoning specialized models arrived, it fell to about 64%.

212
00:18:04,800 --> 00:18:06,520
The data are thin.

213
00:18:06,520 --> 00:18:10,320
Less than half of the model and benchmark combinations were actually measured,

214
00:18:10,360 --> 00:18:12,900
and the latest period has only four models.

215
00:18:13,120 --> 00:18:14,840
So treat it as a suggestion.

216
00:18:15,080 --> 00:18:17,980
But it fits the common cause story from episode one.

217
00:18:18,180 --> 00:18:21,960
When scale was the main thing changing, everything rose together.

218
00:18:22,220 --> 00:18:24,960
As labs began training for particular skills,

219
00:18:25,280 --> 00:18:28,160
the general factor weakened within each new group of models.

220
00:18:28,400 --> 00:18:32,920
Though pooled across all of them, it still accounts for about 90% of the variation.

221
00:18:32,920 --> 00:18:39,820
Which would make machine G an artifact of how the systems were built, not a property of intelligence.

222
00:18:40,260 --> 00:18:43,020
Or a property of one era of building them.

223
00:18:43,340 --> 00:18:50,360
Either way, the structure of machine abilities has shifted within a few years, far faster than anything measured in people.

224
00:18:51,460 --> 00:18:54,360
So after all of this, do we have a definition?

225
00:18:54,780 --> 00:18:57,960
We have several, and they disagree in revealing ways.

226
00:18:57,960 --> 00:19:03,000
The psychometric view defines intelligence by how abilities cavery in people.

227
00:19:03,480 --> 00:19:11,760
The algorithmic view of Solomonoff, Legge and Hutter defines it as expected success across all environments, weighted by simplicity.

228
00:19:12,240 --> 00:19:16,460
Elegant, uncomputable, and relative to a choice of reference machine.

229
00:19:16,980 --> 00:19:20,520
Shale defines it as efficiency of learning relative to priors.

230
00:19:20,520 --> 00:19:29,140
And the practical views define it by what systems can actually do, how long they can work on their own, and how human-like their profile is.

231
00:19:29,400 --> 00:19:32,680
For large language models, these come apart sharply.

232
00:19:33,160 --> 00:19:39,400
Their huge training sets count heavily against them on Chalet's measure and not at all on Legg and Hudders.

233
00:19:39,700 --> 00:19:47,620
Their uneven profile shows up immediately on Hendrix's, and their scores depend on harnesses in ways that make every number contestable.

234
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:52,340
What the definitions share is a core idea that will run through the rest of the series.

235
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:58,660
Intelligence is bound up with prediction, with building models good enough to anticipate what comes next.

236
00:19:59,280 --> 00:20:02,280
Legg and Hutter have not stopped at intelligence, either.

237
00:20:02,740 --> 00:20:09,980
In September 2026, both were among the authors of a framework for assessing whether AI systems might be conscious,

238
00:20:10,260 --> 00:20:12,300
which we'll examine in Episode 12.

239
00:20:12,500 --> 00:20:15,780
But where does the drive to predict come from in the first place?

240
00:20:17,620 --> 00:20:22,440
we go back to the origin of life, and to the idea that life itself is computation.

